{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc | sed -n '1070,1125p;1200,1265p;1435,1510p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc | sed -n '548,582p'; rg -n \\\"pending-run|:base\\\" src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc | tail -45\"","aggregated_output":"  1070\t  \"The message a policy-driven abandon prints, in the shape of the\n  1071\t   `(abandon)` notice, so a policy abandon is never mistaken for a\n  1072\t   user's (yin.repl.link-policy.md section 3.3).\"\n  1073\t  \";; the session link policy ended the require\\n\")\n  1074\t\n  1075\t\n  1076\t(defn- abandon-pending\n  1077\t  \"Give up the shell's `:pending-run` (section 7.2, step 7, the abandoned\n  1078\t   case): every install in flight is dropped through the engine's own\n  1079\t   `refused`, every link wait entry is retired with the reason raised as\n  1080\t   the require's error, and the shell returns to the round the require\n  1081\t   began in, whose store and value history the parked evaluation never\n  1082\t   wrote.  Lines typed while the require was pending are dropped with\n  1083\t   it, and said so.  The linker side learns nothing: no response was\n  1084\t   ever promised, and one that still arrives is skipped as late.  The\n  1085\t   identity the parked evaluation minted -- link ids and child origins\n  1086\t   -- is carried onto the base, so nothing is minted twice on the\n  1087\t   surviving pair.  The `(abandon)` command retires with\n  1088\t   `:yin.repl/abandoned` and no message of its own; the session link\n  1089\t   policy retires with the reason it answered and prints\n  1090\t   `policy-abandon-text`, so a policy abandon is never mistaken for a\n  1091\t   user's.\"\n  1092\t  ([state] (abandon-pending state :yin.repl/abandoned nil))\n  1093\t  ([state reason ended-text]\n  1094\t   (if-let [parked (:pending-run state)]\n  1095\t     (let [[state' text] (drain-output state)\n  1096\t           ;; installs first: 